Example 2: 10 -> 20 -> 30 -> 40 -> 50  (Not Palindrome ✗)

--------------------------------- PASS 1: BUILD STACK ---------------------------------

cur = 10  →  stack.push(10)  →  stack: [10]
cur = 20  →  stack.push(20)  →  stack: [20, 10]
cur = 30  →  stack.push(30)  →  stack: [30, 20, 10]
cur = 40  →  stack.push(40)  →  stack: [40, 30, 20, 10]
cur = 50  →  stack.push(50)  →  stack: [50, 40, 30, 20, 10]  ← TOP

Stack (Top→Bottom): 50, 40, 30, 20, 10


--------------------------------- PASS 2: COMPARE ---------------------------------

cur=10, stack.pop()=50  →  10 != 50  ✗

Mismatch found → Return FALSE ✗


--------------------------------- VISUAL ---------------------------------

Original:  10 → 20 → 30 → 40 → 50
           ↓   ↓   ↓   ↓   ↓
Stack:     50  40  30  20  10  (pops: 50,40,30,20,10)

Reverse:   50 → 40 → 30 → 20 → 10  (different from original)
           ✗

First comparison fails: 10 ≠ 50


--------------------------------- SUMMARY TABLE ---------------------------------

Example    List                        Stack (Top→Bottom)        Result
-------    ----                        -------------------        ------
1          1→2→1→1→2→1                [1,2,1,1,2,1]             TRUE ✓
2          10→20→30→40→50             [50,40,30,20,10]          FALSE ✗


--------------------------------- KEY POINTS ---------------------------------

✓ Stack gives reverse order (LIFO property)
✓ Two passes through the list
✓ Compares original order with reverse order
✓ Returns early on first mismatch (optimization)
✓ Simple and intuitive approach

Time: O(n)  |  Space: O(n)  [Stack stores all n nodes]

//////////////////////// QUESTION ////////////////////////////

Palindrome Linked List
Given the head of a singly linked list, check if it reads the same forward and backward.

Example 1:
Input: 1 → 2 → 1 → 1 → 2 → 1
Output: true
Explanation: Reads same forward and backward.

Example 2:
Input: 10 → 20 → 30 → 40 → 50
Output: false
Explanation: 10→20→30→40→50 ≠ 50→40→30→20→10.

Constraints:
- 1 ≤ number of nodes ≤ 10⁵
- 0 ≤ node->data ≤ 10³

Your Approach: Stack-based comparison
- Pass 1: Push all values to stack (O(n) space)
- Pass 2: Compare forward traversal with stack pops (reverse order)
- Return false on first mismatch, true if all match

Time: O(n) | Space: O(n)
